   "source": [
    "## 15. Conclusions, failure regimes and further work\n",
    "\n",
    "**Results (final full-calendar table, saved run).** Test period: baseline annualised return -1.765%, Sharpe -0.318, volatility 5.189%; confirmation -0.400%, Sharpe -0.073, volatility 4.241%; SPY buy-and-hold 28.818% annualised, Sharpe 1.961, volatility 13.372%. Annualised figures scale a 6.5-month result; section 14b shows the actual period returns (SPY buy-and-hold about +15%). These results do not reproduce the paper's headline returns, which use dynamic leverage over 2007-2024, and they do not establish a profitable edge.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Is confirmation better?** The evidence does not support that conclusion. It lost less than the baseline in the test period (-0.400% vs -1.765%) but more in development (-1.246% vs -0.194%). The paired daily differences are small relative to noise (section 14b), and the lower volatility is largely mechanical, because the rule trades about 35-40% less and is invested less often. The honest statement is: the difference between the two rules is not statistically distinguishable from zero in this sample.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Where the losses come from (descriptive, not a proven cause).** In the test period most of the baseline loss is gross trading loss rather than costs: with all costs set to zero the annualised return is still negative. Short trades lost most while SPY rose over the period. In development, costs turned a roughly break-even gross result into a small net loss. With 459 sessions and fewer than 300 trades per strategy, the data cannot separate \"no edge\" from \"a noisy edge of the size the paper reports\".\n",
    "\n",
    "**When momentum may fail.** The strategy may benefit from sustained intraday trends. It may fail in choppy or reversing markets, with delayed entries or exits, or when execution costs consume small gains. No formal regime classification or causal explanation is estimated here.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations.** Short sample and a single split; fixed sizing instead of volatility targeting; retrospective but harmless early-close exclusion (all five are scheduled early closes); bar-based fills and VWAP; the 15:59 end-of-day exit proxy, whose alternative (official close) shifts results by amounts comparable to the strategy's whole profit and loss (section 14b); omitted borrowing costs, regulatory fees and interest; daily-only drawdown; zero risk-free rate in Sharpe.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Further work.** Obtain a longer history and use walk-forward evaluation; decide the end-of-day fill convention before looking at results; test volatility targeting or minute-level exits only on fresh, unseen data rather than tuning this test period. A time-series model is not recommended unless it can be evaluated on enough new data.\n",
    "\n",
